1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,697
The bus stand Chai Corner
has been open for one month.

2
00:00:03,997 --> 00:00:06,461
The plan was five hundred cups a day.

3
00:00:06,461 --> 00:00:07,789
But is it working?

4
00:00:08,339 --> 00:00:11,143
Some cups are full, and
some are half empty.

5
00:00:11,443 --> 00:00:14,334
Some customers wait one
minute, and some wait five.

6
00:00:14,634 --> 00:00:17,612
And nobody has counted the cups even once.

7
00:00:18,262 --> 00:00:20,971
A plan nobody checks is just a guess.

8
00:00:20,971 --> 00:00:22,629
Ravi needs controlling.

9
00:00:23,329 --> 00:00:24,696
This is controlling.

10
00:00:25,046 --> 00:00:28,910
Every evening, Kabir sits with the
cash box and the ledger, and checks

11
00:00:28,910 --> 00:00:29,810
the numbers.

12
00:00:30,087 --> 00:00:33,864
Controlling means checking that
the real work matches the plan.

13
00:00:34,214 --> 00:00:37,094
And when it does not, finding
out why, and fixing it.

14
00:00:37,744 --> 00:00:40,609
Controlling runs as a loop of five steps.

15
00:00:40,959 --> 00:00:41,859
One.

16
00:00:41,493 --> 00:00:42,833
Set the standard.

17
00:00:42,833 --> 00:00:45,586
The target is five hundred cups a day.

18
00:00:45,936 --> 00:00:46,836
Two.

19
00:00:46,443 --> 00:00:48,025
Measure the real work.

20
00:00:48,025 --> 00:00:50,422
Kabir counts the cups every evening.

21
00:00:50,772 --> 00:00:51,672
Three.

22
00:00:51,359 --> 00:00:52,259
Compare.

23
00:00:52,042 --> 00:00:54,313
Outlet two sells only four hundred.

24
00:00:54,313 --> 00:00:56,093
That is one hundred short.

25
00:00:56,443 --> 00:00:57,343
Four.

26
00:00:56,971 --> 00:00:58,084
Find the cause.

27
00:00:58,084 --> 00:01:02,404
At the morning rush, one stove is
not enough, and people walk away.

28
00:01:02,754 --> 00:01:03,654
Five.

29
00:01:03,339 --> 00:01:04,299
Correct it.

30
00:01:04,299 --> 00:01:08,704
Ravi adds a second stove, and
sales climb back to five hundred.

31
00:01:09,054 --> 00:01:10,691
Then the loop starts again.

32
00:01:10,691 --> 00:01:13,327
Measure, compare, and
correct, every month.

33
00:01:13,977 --> 00:01:16,613
Planning and controlling
always go together.

34
00:01:16,963 --> 00:01:18,437
Planning sets the target.

35
00:01:18,437 --> 00:01:20,351
Controlling checks if you hit it.

36
00:01:20,701 --> 00:01:23,680
That is why they are called
the twins of management.

37
00:01:24,330 --> 00:01:26,876
Good managers control at three moments.

38
00:01:27,226 --> 00:01:28,413
Before the work.

39
00:01:28,413 --> 00:01:31,635
Meena checks that the milk
is fresh before brewing.

40
00:01:31,635 --> 00:01:33,459
This is feedforward control.

41
00:01:33,809 --> 00:01:35,011
During the work.

42
00:01:35,011 --> 00:01:37,425
Asha tastes the chai while it boils.

43
00:01:37,425 --> 00:01:39,224
This is concurrent control.

44
00:01:39,574 --> 00:01:40,725
After the work.

45
00:01:40,725 --> 00:01:42,628
Kabir counts the day's sales.

46
00:01:42,628 --> 00:01:44,326
This is feedback control.

47
00:01:44,976 --> 00:01:47,692
But Ravi cannot check
every small thing himself.

48
00:01:48,042 --> 00:01:52,293
So small slips are fixed by the
team, and only big gaps reach him.

49
00:01:52,643 --> 00:01:53,543
F.W.

50
00:01:53,228 --> 00:01:56,041
Taylor wrote about this in 1903.

51
00:01:56,041 --> 00:01:59,172
He called it the exception principle.

52
00:01:59,822 --> 00:02:02,620
So what does controlling
look like at work today?

53
00:02:02,970 --> 00:02:03,870
Standards.

54
00:02:03,765 --> 00:02:08,250
A delivery company promises a time,
and tracks every order against it.

55
00:02:08,600 --> 00:02:09,500
Measuring.

56
00:02:09,401 --> 00:02:13,072
A factory counts faulty parts
at the end of every shift.

57
00:02:13,422 --> 00:02:14,322
Comparing.

58
00:02:14,205 --> 00:02:18,335
Each month, a manager puts the
budget next to the real spending.

59
00:02:18,685 --> 00:02:19,585
Feedforward.

60
00:02:19,516 --> 00:02:22,698
Before every flight, pilots
work through a checklist.

61
00:02:23,048 --> 00:02:23,948
Exception.

62
00:02:23,885 --> 00:02:28,037
A bank flags only the unusual
payments for a person to check.

63
00:02:28,687 --> 00:02:30,513
But can control go too far?

64
00:02:30,513 --> 00:02:31,413
Yes.

65
00:02:31,338 --> 00:02:32,386
The good side.

66
00:02:32,386 --> 00:02:36,856
Problems are caught early, less is
wasted, and everyone knows the target.

67
00:02:37,206 --> 00:02:38,278
The hard side.

68
00:02:38,278 --> 00:02:42,024
Too much checking feels like
being watched, and trust drops.

69
00:02:42,374 --> 00:02:45,422
And people start chasing the
number, instead of the goal.

70
00:02:45,772 --> 00:02:50,389
If Ravi only counts cups, a helper
might fill cups half way, just to sell

71
00:02:50,389 --> 00:02:51,289
more.

72
00:02:51,278 --> 00:02:56,284
The British economist Charles
Goodhart warned about this in 1975.

73
00:02:56,284 --> 00:02:58,921
It is now called Goodhart's law.

74
00:02:59,271 --> 00:03:00,888
So the lesson is balance.

75
00:03:00,888 --> 00:03:03,555
Measure what matters,
and trust your people.

76
00:03:04,205 --> 00:03:05,110
Quick check.

77
00:03:05,110 --> 00:03:08,896
Asha tastes the chai while it
boils, before anyone drinks it.

78
00:03:08,896 --> 00:03:10,865
Which type of control is this?

79
00:03:12,765 --> 00:03:13,908
Concurrent control!

80
00:03:13,908 --> 00:03:16,316
It checks the work while it is happening.

81
00:03:16,916 --> 00:03:17,925
So, remember.

82
00:03:18,225 --> 00:03:22,444
Controlling checks the real work
against the plan, and fixes the gap.

83
00:03:22,794 --> 00:03:26,500
Set the standard, measure, compare,
find the cause, and correct.

84
00:03:26,850 --> 00:03:31,805
Control before, during, and after the
work, and focus on the big exceptions.

85
00:03:32,405 --> 00:03:35,713
Next time, Ravi and his
team set goals together.

86
00:03:35,713 --> 00:03:37,764
That's management by objectives.
